Scope: git diff master -- web/templates/components/kpis.html, internal/handlers/dashboard/
handlers.go, internal/services/metrics/metrics.go (fix/phase-visibility, W3.2 manifest).
Audited in a fresh throwaway copy of /home/darrell/bin/ai/budget2 (cp -a, .git kept) at
/home/darrell/work/agents2/.swarm-work/budget2-w3-2. No project file was edited in the real
tree; only the verdict file above was written there. Same real-handler render harness as
attempt 1 (existing route-level tests plus two temporary local dump tests hitting the same
/dashboard/kpis route, deleted after use — not part of the accepted diff).

Diff under test (kpis.html), condensed:
  {{- $prov := .TargetProvenance}}
  {{- $targetFigure := formatMoney .Metrics.BudgetTarget}}
  {{- if $prov.Annotate}}
    {{if $prov.Straddles}}
      $targetAnnotation = "Target %s — from What-If plan: %s base × avg %s (crosses phase change)"
    {{else}}
      $targetAnnotation = "Target %s — from What-If plan: %s base × %s (%s phase)"
    {{end}}
  {{end}}
  {{if $prov.Annotate}}<span title="{{$targetAnnotation}}" aria-label="{{$targetAnnotation}}">
      Target <span class="num">{{formatMoney .Metrics.BudgetTarget}}</span></span>
  {{else}}Target <span class="num">{{formatMoney .Metrics.BudgetTarget}}</span>{{end}}

$targetFigure is produced by the exact same template call, `formatMoney .Metrics.BudgetTarget`,
that renders the visible number in the nested <span class="num">, on the same render pass —
so the two cannot drift by construction.

CHECK 1 — accessible name now contains the figure AND provenance (1.3.1 / 4.1.2): PASS.
Rendered via the project's own test, TestDashboardKPIs_TargetProvenance_AnnotatesWhenPhaseActive
(handlers_http_test.go:1521, base $2,000, Active phase, multiplier 0.95):
  title="Target $1,900.00 — from What-If plan: $2,000 base × 0.95 (Active phase)"
  aria-label="Target $1,900.00 — from What-If plan: $2,000 base × 0.95 (Active phase)"
`go test ./internal/handlers/dashboard/... -run TestDashboardKPIs_TargetProvenance -v` — PASS
(both provenance tests).
Independently confirmed via a temporary local dump test reproducing the task's own example
fixture (base $7,386, Go-Go phase, multiplier 1.1, one-month range):
  <p class="text-sm emerald...">
    <span title="Target $8,124.60 — from What-If plan: $7,386 base × 1.1 (Go-Go phase)"
          aria-label="Target $8,124.60 — from What-If plan: $7,386 base × 1.1 (Go-Go phase)">
      Target <span class="num">$8,124.60</span>
    </span> · <span class="num">$6,651.82</span>/mo under
  </p>
Visible figure ($8,124.60) and aria-label's leading figure are byte-identical. AT users now
hear the actual target dollar figure plus its provenance; the content loss from attempt 1
(Check 1 FAIL — aria-label omitted the computed target, exposing only the pre-multiplier base)
is resolved. This satisfies 1.3.1 (programmatically-determined text now carries the
visually-presented value) and 4.1.2 (exposed name supplements rather than replaces the
rendered figure).
Also exercised the straddling-range branch with a second temporary dump test (base $2,000,
range crossing a phase boundary, weighted-average multiplier):
  title="Target $1,984.62 — from What-If plan: $2,000 base × avg 0.99 (crosses phase change)"
  aria-label= (identical)
  visible: <span class="num">$1,984.62</span>
Figure again matches exactly; "crosses phase change" wording linearizes acceptably for AT
(Check 5).

CHECK 2 — title == aria-label, no title-only info (1.4.13 / 2.1.1): PASS.
Both attributes interpolate the same {{$targetAnnotation}} value in all three fixtures
exercised above (single-phase Active, single-phase Go-Go, straddling). Byte-identical in
every rendered case. Same OBSERVATION as attempt 1 stands (non-blocking, unchanged by this
fix): the native title tooltip is mouse-hover-only since the span isn't focusable, so
sighted keyboard-only users still get neither channel. Not a new regression and not an AA
violation on its own (1.4.13 governs parity of trigger *content* where hover-or-focus content
exists, not a mandate that both triggers exist).

CHECK 3 — no wrapper span / no attributes in the unannotated case (byte-for-byte AT parity
with pre-feature master): PASS.
`{{if $prov.Annotate}}<span ...>...</span>{{else}}Target <span class="num">...</span>{{end}}`
— the wrapper span exists only inside the Annotate branch; the else branch is exactly the
original unwrapped markup. Confirmed by running the project's own
TestDashboardKPIs_TargetProvenance_AbsentWhenNoPhaseConfig (handlers_http_test.go:1576),
which asserts no "Target from What-If plan:" text, no `aria-label="Target` attribute, and
(via regex tolerant of surrounding whitespace) the bare `Target <span class="num">` markup
with no wrapper — PASS. Note: the $prov/$targetFigure/$targetAnnotation `{{- ...}}` assignment
lines execute whenever `.Metrics.HasBudgetTarget` is true (even when Annotate is false),
each leaving one blank output line because they use only left-trim (`{{-`), not `-}}`. This
is a whitespace-only artifact — confirmed by inspection that it does not add nodes, text, or
attributes to the DOM and cannot change the accessible-name computation (browsers collapse
whitespace-only text nodes) — so it does not affect what AT announces. Not a FAIL; noted as
an OBSERVATION below.

CHECK 4 — still no tabindex/focusability introduced: PASS.
Diff adds only `title=` / `aria-label=` (and, in the annotated branch, the wrapping `<span>`
itself) — no `tabindex`, `role`, or interactive markup anywhere in the diff. The span remains
correctly out of the tab order.

CHECK 5 — em-dash separator linearizes acceptably: PASS.
Rendered strings ("Target $8,124.60 — from What-If plan: ...", "Target $1,984.62 — from
What-If plan: ... (crosses phase change)") place the em-dash between two complete clauses
(figure, then provenance clause) with a space on each side. Screen readers either announce it
as a pause/"dash" or silently drop it depending on verbosity settings; either way the two
clauses remain distinguishable and neither reading loses information. No WCAG criterion
requires a specific punctuation-to-speech mapping here.

ACCESSIBILITY.md cross-check: point 4 (aria-label supplementing/standing in for a visible
label without erasing visually-presented content) and point 13 (accessible text must carry
what sighted users see, not substitute a different figure) are now satisfied — the aria-label
leads with the exact visible target figure before adding provenance, rather than replacing it.

Build sanity: `go build ./...` clean in the audited copy after the fix (no template/handler
compile errors introduced).

OBSERVATIONS (non-blocking, for the lead):
1. (Carried from attempt 1, unchanged) The provenance breakdown is exposed via `title`
   (mouse-hover-only, span not focusable) and `aria-label` (screen-reader-only); sighted
   keyboard-only users get neither. Not an AA violation but worth a design note if keyboard
   users are expected to want the breakdown too (e.g. a visible disclosure control).
2. The `{{- $prov := ...}}`-style assignment lines emit one blank output line each regardless
   of the Annotate branch taken, whenever a budget target exists. Purely cosmetic in rendered
   HTML (collapsed by browsers, verified not to affect the DOM structure or AT output) — flag
   only if the lead cares about minimizing generated whitespace.

VERDICT: PASS — the Check 1 FAIL from attempt 1 (aria-label replacing rather than
supplementing the visible target figure) is resolved: the accessible name now leads with the
exact rendered dollar figure before adding provenance, confirmed across single-phase and
straddling-range fixtures via the real handler/template render path. Checks 2-5 remain PASS
with no new defects introduced.
